WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.543
PACT helps a coordinator recover a shared project when one partner withdraws. In this synthetic

00:00:05.543 --> 00:00:10.837
example, a school needs twenty prepared laptops by three P M. No organization can finish

00:00:10.837 --> 00:00:16.130
the project alone. We start with the original checked plan, which delivers at two P

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:16.483
M.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:24.047
FalkorDB connects the school goal to requirements, offers, and the organizations behind them. These are

00:00:24.047 --> 00:00:30.614
live graph facts: quantities, availability, processing rates, and completed work. The planner checks connected partner

00:00:30.614 --> 00:00:37.181
combinations with a constraint solver, then independently checks the resulting schedule. The graph is the

00:00:37.181 --> 00:00:39.808
only source of current collaboration state.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:46.480
Now the original wiping provider withdraws after ten devices are finished. PACT preserves those ten

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.150
records and considers two backup offers for the rest. W two can finish in time,

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:57.820
with delivery at two thirty. W three is also connected, but its later availability misses

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.467
the deadline. The rejected alternative stays visible.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:07.187
The local coordinator agent calls three read-only tools through MCP: inspect connections, check schedule, and

00:01:07.187 --> 00:01:12.904
recall failure. An old W two failure at five devices an hour does not match

00:01:12.904 --> 00:01:18.621
its current rate of ten. After checking the evidence, the agent requests a pending proposal.

00:01:18.621 --> 00:01:22.052
This is human review, never an approval or reservation.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:28.684
Change W two back to its old rate. Both backup schedules now fail, and PACT

00:01:28.684 --> 00:01:34.318
blocks the proposal. The historical warning becomes applicable. The earlier saved proposal is marked stale

00:01:34.318 --> 00:01:39.951
because the graph revision changed. PACT keeps the ten completed records instead of pretending the

00:01:39.951 --> 00:01:41.078
project starts over.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:47.915
Move W three earlier while W two remains slow. PACT now finds a checked two

00:01:47.915 --> 00:01:53.750
P M continuation. The first-active-offer baseline still blocks. Both policies use the same current facts

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:59.196
and schedule checker. This is a bounded synthetic comparison, not a general performance claim.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:05.673
The custom form accepts actual offer terms, not just named presets. Set W two to

00:02:05.673 --> 00:02:11.146
seven devices per hour and W three to ten fifteen. These values are written to

00:02:11.146 --> 00:02:16.619
FalkorDB. The same planner selects W three for two fifteen. The change ledger records which

00:02:16.619 --> 00:02:19.903
offer terms changed and the previous proposal remains stale.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:26.962
The public source includes the graph model, executed Cypher, setup instructions, and verification suite. The

00:02:26.962 --> 00:02:33.023
reset fix checks a unique case identity as well as its revision, rejecting old proposals

00:02:33.023 --> 00:02:39.085
even when revision numbers repeat. PACT is one synthetic workflow with checked pending proposals. Codex

00:02:39.085 --> 00:02:45.146
coding assistance and this computer narration are disclosed. The entry targets Agents That Act on

00:02:45.146 --> 00:02:45.955
Connected Data.
